  (:adr/body
  "# ADR-2608082248: Kotoba Capability Machine (KCM)

## Decision

The preferred Murakumo coding-agent execution profile is the **Kotoba Capability
Machine**. KCM is not a smaller Unix VM. It is a closed, content-addressed
Kotoba machine contract:

`definition closure CID + compiler CID + module-lock CID + target ABI + HostCaps
policy CID + provider closure SHA-256 + Node runtime SHA-256 + declared Kotoba
check arguments = machine id`.

The model receives typed code capabilities only (`code/list`, `code/read`,
`code/write`, `code/edit`, `build/check`, `build/compile`). It never receives shell, process
spawn, an executable name, or argv. `kotoba_check` accepts only a declared check
id. The host never resolves `kotoba` from PATH. It verifies a provider closure
containing compiler source, the git-locked dependency graph, NBB and its npm
runtime dependencies, then executes the manifest entry plus committed arguments
without `sh -c`. Every regular file, the transport archive, and the selected
Node runtime bytes are checked before execution and again before cache reuse.
Declared compiles are a separate `kotoba_build` tool and are never result-only
cached, because a cache hit must not claim an artifact it did not restore.

Seatbelt remains the outer OS containment backing. It is not the Kotoba language
security model. Container or microVM backing may replace it for a high-risk
tenant without changing the KCM identity or guest capability surface.

## Implementation

Implemented in `kotoba-lang/kotoba-fleet` from manifest pin `a2ed736`:

- `hosts/nbb/fleet/kcm.cljs`: canonical contract, validation, machine/cache
  identities and capability-to-tool projection.
- `kcm_provider.cljs`: archive traversal/type checks, exact manifest/tree/file
  verification, Node runtime verification and provider argv construction.
- `sandbox_agent.cljs`: KCM-only tool surface, exact argv execution without a
  shell, complete provider verification, final authoritative check pass,
  persistent pure-check cache and cache evidence.
- `dispatch.cljs`: copies the KCM contract runtime to the Murakumo node and
  forwards only the KCM fields.
- `gate.cljs`: independently recomputes and admits the dispatched KCM identity.
- `receipt.cljs`: signs machine id, contract, capabilities and cache evidence.
- `node.cljs`: measures Node bytes and constrains placement to the runtime bound
  into the KCM identity; a system `kotoba` command is neither required nor used.
- `scripts/build-kcm-provider.cljs`: builds a stable 566-file closure manifest
  and ordinary tar transport from the compiler pin and dependency lock.
- `scripts/kcm-coldstart-probe.cljs`: real compiler cold/warm and corruption
  negative-control probe.
- `scripts/kcm-node-probe.cljs`: ephemeral `/tmp` fleet-node execution probe.

The compiler pin had a stale `deps-lock.edn` (digest `0126…` vs current
`deps.edn` `0993…`). The canonical lock generator refreshed three advanced
dependency pins; the JDK-free `bin/kotoba -M check` path is green again.

Legacy sandbox work-units remain available for migration and are explicitly
reported as `legacy-sandbox`; they cannot satisfy a KCM work-unit at the gate.

## Cache and cold start

Only checks declared `pure?` are cacheable, and an identity declaring effects
cannot mark checks pure. The cache key binds the entire machine id, exact check
descriptor and patch digest. Compiler, closure, lock, ABI, HostCaps policy,
provider closure, runtime, command or edited code changes therefore miss. The cache lives
outside the ephemeral checkout but agent-authored code cannot reach it through
the Seatbelt profile.

Local measured real-provider probe on 2026-08-08 built 566 files / 6,965,123
bytes with closure `39f3480c…`. `examples/list.kotoba` checked successfully: a
cold miss took 4,024 ms and a verified cache hit 141 ms; a wasm32-wasi compile
took 3,182 ms and produced `main.wasm`. A modified archive was rejected before
execution.

The same closure was copied ephemerally to `naphtali` (no installation and no
PATH provider). Against Node `463b47d6…`, the check miss took 904 ms, the
verified hit 37 ms, and wasm32-wasi compile took 715 ms. Seatbelt blocked 6/6
escapes. The remote root was removed after the probe.

## Verification

- portable fleet core: 24 tests / 61 assertions green.
- host selftest: all checks green, including 8-process single-winner lease,
  governor isolation, signed receipts, path confinement, six blocked escapes,
  KCM hash stability, compiler/policy/provider identity invalidation, shell and
  process rejection, tool-surface restriction, cache invalidation, and governor
  rejection of substituted/legacy machines.
- local real-provider probe: Kotoba admission green, miss 4,024 ms then hit
  141 ms with identical cache key; wasm compile 3,182 ms; modified archive rejected.
- naphtali real-provider probe: Kotoba admission green, miss 904 ms then hit
  37 ms; wasm compile 715 ms; exact Node bytes admitted; Seatbelt blocked 6/6 escape probes.
- modified host namespaces load under nbb; `git diff --check` clean.

## Remaining rollout boundary

This checkout proves compiler execution on a real Murakumo node but does not
claim a production agent/model turn, governor materialization, or published
signed receipt. Production rollout must publish the provider archive/manifest
to durable content-addressed storage, select only nodes whose measured runtime
matches the KCM identity, then run dispatch → gate → receipt. The implementation
fails placement or execution instead of falling back to JVM, shell, PATH, or an
unpinned provider.")
